  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread frost is expected for northern areas\non Saturday night, with patchy frost elsewhere in interior \nDowneast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n1030mb high centered over southern Ontario will continue \nbuilding, crossing the northern half of the CWA overnight \ntonight. This will lead to clear skies and calm winds, \nespecially directly under the high, which will be prime \nconditions for efficient radiational cooling and temperatures \ndropping below freezing, especially in the north directly under \nthe high. \n\nDowneast may be able to maintain a gradient but interior Downeast \nmay be able to decouple briefly for an hour or two. Thus have min \nforecasts around 37 over central Washington and interior Hancock \nwhich may be enough for frost to develop and have issued a Frost \nAdvisory from 07-12z tonight. Coastal Downeast will remain in the \nu30s to lwr 40s. \n\nThe frost/freeze program has ended for the entire CWA aside for \nHancock and Washington counties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another round of rain is possible Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA vertically stacked system will swing over James Bay Sunday \nnight. Weak lopres will be moving south of Cape Cod with \npossible showers spreading over the waters and possibly into \nDowneast though will likely see dry air hold on long enuf with \nridge axis along the coast to prevent much measurable precip \nfrom falling. \n\nThe bigger story will be the cold front sweeping through the area on \nMonday afternoon. The weak low over the waters should move fast \nenough to prevent interaction with the approaching system from the \nnorthwest. 300mb speed max should provide enuf lift in the left \nfront exit region to enhance rainfall totals over the north, \ngenerally between 0.30-0.50 inches. This will likely have little \nimpact on the ongoing drought. Coastal low deepens as it heads into \nNova Scotia and as it does so northwest winds increase just above \nthe surface with gusts approaching 30 mph late in the afternoon and \nevening.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong westerly winds on Tuesday could lead to\nsome isolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nIncreasing confidence on Tuesday that, as the low pressure \nsystem deepens in the Northern Maritimes, we can expect strong \nsurface high pressure to dive over the eastern Great Lakes. This\nwill lead to a strong pressure gradient between the low and \nhigh, resulting in gusty west winds developing during the \nafternoon and evening hours on Monday. Tuesday probabilities for\n35+ mph wind gusts have stayed consistent to 70-90%, with 40+ \nmph probabilities now 40-70% for Millinocket and areas north. \nFor the Bangor region and interior Downeast, 35+ mph wind gusts \nprobabilities are 20-50%. 40+ mph probabilities have decreased \nto 0-20%. There is ensemble guidance suggesting a potential of \nwind advisory headlines (31-39 mph sustained or gusts 45+ mph). \nUncertainty remains in wind speeds due to uncertainty with \ncloud cover throughout the day on Tuesday. However, it is likely\nthat significant leaf loss may be possible in areas of Northern\nMaine where peak foliage has been reached. Additional leaf loss\nis possible in the Central Highlands to Downeast Maine where \nthe foliage is moderate to high. Gusty winds could also lead to \nsome isolated power outages. Continue to monitor the forecast \nfor updates, as models get a better handle on the atmospheric \nset up.",
